\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Two-dimensional cross-section illustrating the packing topology of gravitational field regions. Each circle represents a locally-coupled field region; lines from the highlighted region (red) indicate coupling to its four nearest neighbors (orange). This square coordination is shown in two dimensions for clarity; in three spatial dimensions, the simplex theorem (Section~\ref {subsec:k4}) requires $k = d + 1 = 4$ boundary faces to enclose a volume, producing tetrahedral rather than square coordination. The essential feature is preserved: the gravitational field consists of distributed regions whose local coupling creates a definite topology.}}{11}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
